1
00:00:11,240 --> 00:00:12,760
Hey everyone, I've got another quick

2
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:15,200
footnote for you between chapters today.

3
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:16,040
When I've talked about linear

4
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:17,840
transformation so far, I've only really

5
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:19,560
talked about transformations from 2D

6
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:21,840
vectors to other 2D vectors represented

7
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:24,600
with 2 by 2 matrices or from 3D vectors

8
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:27,000
to other 3D vectors represented with 3

9
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
by 3 matrices.

10
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,640
But several commenters have asked about

11
00:00:30,640 --> 00:00:32,360
non-square matrices, so I thought I'd

12
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:33,680
take a moment to just show what those

13
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:35,280
mean geometrically.

14
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:37,040
By now in the series, you actually have

15
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:38,600
most of the background you need to start

16
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:40,120
pondering a question like this on your

17
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:41,640
own, but I'll start talking through it

18
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:44,040
just to give a little mental momentum.

19
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:45,640
It's perfectly reasonable to talk about

20
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:48,520
transformations between dimensions, such

21
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:50,760
as one that takes 2D vectors to 3D

22
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:53,280
vectors. Again, what makes one of these

23
00:00:53,280 --> 00:00:55,200
linear is that grid lines remain

24
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:57,440
parallel and evenly spaced and that the

25
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:00,000
origin maps to the origin.

26
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,520
What I have pictured here is the input

27
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:03,200
space on the left, which is just 2D

28
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:04,839
space, and the output of the

29
00:01:04,839 --> 00:01:06,960
transformation shown on the right.

30
00:01:06,960 --> 00:01:08,440
The reason I'm not showing the inputs

31
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:10,280
move over to the outputs like I usually

32
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:12,840
do is not just animation laziness. It's

33
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:15,160
worth emphasizing that 2D vector inputs

34
00:01:15,160 --> 00:01:17,200
are very different animals from these 3D

35
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:19,240
vector outputs living in a completely

36
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:21,720
separate unconnected space.

37
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:23,200
Encoding one of these transformations

38
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,440
with a matrix is really just the same

39
00:01:25,440 --> 00:01:27,360
thing as what we've done before. You

40
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:29,440
look at where each basis vector lands

41
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:31,160
and write the coordinates of the landing

42
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:33,760
spots as the columns of a matrix. For

43
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:35,840
example, what you're looking at here is

44
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:37,920
an output of a transformation that takes

45
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:42,360
I hat to the coordinates 2 -1 -2 and J

46
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:47,240
hat to the coordinates 0 1 1.

47
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:49,800
Notice, this means the matrix encoding

48
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:52,200
our transformation has three rows and

49
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:54,160
two columns, which to use standard

50
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:57,760
terminology makes it a 3 by 2 matrix.

51
00:01:57,760 --> 00:01:59,640
In the language of last video, the

52
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:01,920
column space of this matrix, the place

53
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:04,120
where all the vectors land, is a 2D

54
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:06,320
plane slicing through the origin of 3D

55
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:07,360
space.

56
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:09,679
But the matrix is still full rank since

57
00:02:09,679 --> 00:02:11,560
the number of dimensions in this column

58
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:13,440
space is the same as the number of

59
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:17,400
dimensions of the input space.

60
00:02:17,400 --> 00:02:19,360
So, if you see a 3 by 2 matrix out in

61
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:21,160
the wild, you can know that it has the

62
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:23,480
geometric interpretation of mapping two

63
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:26,040
dimensions to three dimensions since the

64
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:27,760
two columns indicate that the input

65
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:30,040
space has two basis vectors and the

66
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,240
three rows indicate that the landing

67
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:34,760
spots for each of those basis vectors is

68
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:36,240
described with three separate

69
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:37,840
coordinates.

70
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:40,120
Likewise, if you see a 2 by 3 matrix

71
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:42,200
with two rows and three columns, what do

72
00:02:42,200 --> 00:02:43,640
you think that means?

73
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:45,640
Well, the three columns indicate that

74
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:46,960
you're starting in a space that has

75
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:48,880
three basis vectors, so we're starting

76
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:51,200
in three dimensions. And the two rows

77
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:53,120
indicate that the landing spot for each

78
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:55,120
of those three basis vectors is

79
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:57,600
described with only two coordinates, so

80
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,440
they must be landing in two dimensions.

81
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:02,920
So, it's a transformation from 3D space

82
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:05,400
onto the 2D plane.

83
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:07,200
A transformation that should feel very

84
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:08,800
uncomfortable if you imagine going

85
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:11,360
through it.

86
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:15,160
You could also have a transformation

87
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:17,800
from two dimensions to one dimension.

88
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:19,440
One-dimensional space is really just the

89
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:21,280
number line, so a transformation like

90
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:24,320
this takes in 2D vectors and spits out

91
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:25,720
numbers.

92
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:27,400
Thinking about grid lines remaining

93
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:29,160
parallel and evenly spaced is a little

94
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:30,560
bit messy due to all of the

95
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:32,480
squishification happening here. So, in

96
00:03:32,480 --> 00:03:34,560
this case, the visual understanding for

97
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:36,800
what linearity means is that if you have

98
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:39,320
a line of evenly spaced dots, it would

99
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:41,360
remain evenly spaced once they're mapped

100
00:03:41,360 --> 00:03:43,680
onto the number line.

101
00:03:43,680 --> 00:03:45,520
One of these transformations is encoded

102
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:48,160
with a 1 by 2 matrix, each of whose two

103
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:51,080
columns has just a single entry. The two

104
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:52,520
columns represent where the basis

105
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:54,320
vectors land and each one of those

106
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:56,440
columns requires just one number, the

107
00:03:56,440 --> 00:03:59,200
number that that basis vector landed on.

108
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:00,640
This is actually a surprisingly

109
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:02,520
meaningful type of transformation with

110
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:04,280
close ties to the dot product and I'll

111
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:06,280
be talking about that next video.

112
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:07,920
Until then, I encourage you to play

113
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:09,640
around with this idea on your own

114
00:04:09,640 --> 00:04:11,000
contemplating the meanings of things

115
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:12,840
like matrix multiplication and linear

116
00:04:12,840 --> 00:04:15,000
systems of equations in the context of

117
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:16,959
transformations between different

118
00:04:16,959 --> 00:04:20,560
dimensions. Have fun.